 "result": "I created two files: `Dockerfile` and `.dockerignore`. I couldn't run any commands here, so the image hasn't been built or tested.\n\n**`Dockerfile`** (three build steps):\n1. **Web page:** builds the React frontend in `frontend/` from its lockfile.\n2. **Server dependencies:** installs the Express server's runtime dependencies only (no dev dependencies).\n3. **Final image:** a small Node 20 image that copies in the server code, `customQuotes/`, the dependencies and the built frontend. It runs as a non-root user, listens on port 3002 and starts with `node -r dotenv/config server.js`. It sets `PORT` and `SERVICE_URL` to the values from the repo's `.env`, so it works without extra settings. You can pass `UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY` when starting the container if you want Unsplash backgrounds.\n\n**`.dockerignore`** leaves out `node_modules`, any existing frontend build, git files, docs and images, and `.env`. Leaving out `.env` keeps any API key you add there out of the image. You can still mount one at `/app/.env` when you run the container.\n\n**Decision for you:** the existing `npm start` / `npm run prod` sets `NODE_ENV=PRODUCTION`. In that mode, `src/app.js:25` sends the web page for every GET request, and that rule comes before the API routes are set up. So `/quote`, `/image` and `/api-docs` would all return the web page instead of working. The image therefore defaults to `NODE_ENV=production` (lowercase), which keeps the API working. To serve the web page instead, start the container with `-e NODE_ENV=PRODUCTION`, but the API will be unreachable in that mode.\n\nExample:\n```\ndocker build -t readme-quotes .\ndocker run -p 3002:3002 readme-quotes\n# http://localhost:3002/quote?quoteCategory=programming\n```",
